                <pb facs="0544" n="464" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg464"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Lawrence to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >House of Representatives</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">North
                                Adams, Mass.</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-10-13"><hi rendition="#italic">October 13,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Sir</hi>: I inclose herewith a letter from
                    Mr. John J. Hearn, of Westfield, Mass., a constituent of mine of character and
                    standing, who writes to me with reference to the release of his brother, F. M.
                    Hearn, now a Boer prisoner of war in the hands of the British. Will you please
                    inform me if anything can be done by your Department for Mr. Hearn? I shall be
                    glad to have you do all that you can.</p>
                <p>Very truly, yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Geo. P. Lawrence.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
